WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.420
- Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.910
Last month, the cast of
the incredible film "CODA"

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:07.240
became the first
predominantly deaf cast ever

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.430
to win outstanding performance
by a cast in a motion picture

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:11.890
at the SAG awards.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:12.889
Incredible.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:14.335
(audience applauds)

00:00:14.335 --> 00:00:16.420
The film is also up for best picture

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.620
at the Oscars this year.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.180
I will be shocked and
floored if they don't win.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.050
There's so much talent,
there's so many great movies,

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.370
but this movie literally

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.980
is one of my favorite
movies I've ever seen.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.350
You can catch it streaming now

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.940
on Apple TV plus if you're smart.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.730
Say hello to the cast of "CODA"

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.540
Daniel Durant, Troy Kotsur,
Marlee Matlin, Emilia Jones.

00:00:37.414 --> 00:00:42.414
(upbeat music)
(audience applauds)

00:00:51.002 --> 00:00:52.085
Hello, hello.

00:00:54.808 --> 00:00:56.517
Nice to see you.

00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:57.350
Oh, hello.

00:01:04.591 --> 00:01:05.520
I will tell you,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.240
I have interviewed so many people.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.090
I'm so lucky this job has been...

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.370
I've been so fortunate to
get to meet a lot of people

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.330
that I love and inspire me.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.100
I have been asking my producer

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.400
when this show is going to
happen for I don't know how long.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.010
I loved the film.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.430
It was so good.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.220
It was so moving.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.650
I laughed, I cried, I was inspired,

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:27.483
it was thought provoking.

00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:30.200
There were so many things
happening with the film,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.810
and it's great to have you here.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.960
Thank you for taking the time.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.880
You're so busy winning awards.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.660
But it's great to see you again.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.563
Marlee, thank you for coming back.

00:01:41.563 --> 00:01:43.930
- [Marlee's Translator]
So, I was counting the days

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.280
when I was gonna come
back to see you again.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.730
Thank you for having us here,

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.150
and I love your show so much.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.090
I love you, love you, love you.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.980
And speaking about I love you,

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.600
we are giving you a gift here.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.550
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:55.947
What?

00:01:55.947 --> 00:01:57.580
- [Marlee's Translator]
Here, this is for you.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.753
This is a very special
gift that was made for you.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.700
I'm giving to you by my best friend.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.140
Her name is Alexis Kashar.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.700
- [Kelly] My makeup
artist is gonna hate me.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:10.623
That's so sweet.

00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:13.440
- [Marlee's Translator] And
it's the sign for I love you.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.820
It's a pendant.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.640
- That's so sweet.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.856
(audience applauds)

00:02:19.856 --> 00:02:21.765
- [Marlee's Translator]
It's a line of jewelry

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:22.975
called RoseBYANDER.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:26.972
So that's the name of the jewelry
and it's just, I love you.

00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:27.920
A one of a kind special jewelry.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.430
- It's amazing 'cause
it's coming from you.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.720
It's also a very special meaning.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.050
Side note for me and my
daughter that I won't say on air

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.080
because I'll cry.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.150
But anyway, there's something
special with this sign with me

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:37.983
and my kid as well.

00:02:37.983 --> 00:02:38.816
So this is very beautiful.

00:02:38.816 --> 00:02:40.422
Thank you so much.

00:02:40.422 --> 00:02:42.560
(audience applauds)

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.003
Had any of y'all had
met before this movie?

00:02:46.203 --> 00:02:47.036
- [Marlee's Translator] Yeah, we did.

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:47.869
We knew each other.

00:02:47.869 --> 00:02:48.702
We all knew each other.

00:02:48.702 --> 00:02:49.855
The three of us knew each other,

00:02:49.855 --> 00:02:51.760
but actually working as
intensely as we did on this film,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.410
no, we didn't actually.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.040
So I'm so honored to have
worked with the two of them.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.610
They're so talented deaf actors,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.080
and it was really more special

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.450
to have Emilia on board with us as well.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:04.720
This is the perfect cast.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.996
You'll never see a more perfect cast.

00:03:06.996 --> 00:03:09.017
(audience applauds)

00:03:09.017 --> 00:03:10.610
- I could not agree more.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.940
It was, and I've never seen it on film.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.570
That's why I think this
is so important in film.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.100
I've never seen a full cast like this.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.650
I've never really had to
watch or pay attention

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.130
in a different way.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.080
It was such a beautiful
and needed experience

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.770
that I did not know that I needed.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.830
And I did not know that
American sign language,

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.600
it's so passionate.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.210
It's a passionate language.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.350
I mean, it's just a full body.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:34.330
You're not just using your voice.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:35.540
It's a full body experience

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.200
of how deeply hurt you are,
or how deeply in love you are,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.050
or, you know, frustrated you are.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.521
It was just such an
amazing thing to watch.

00:03:43.521 --> 00:03:44.354
- [Marlee's Translator] Exactly.

00:03:44.354 --> 00:03:47.090
It's because I mean, it's two things.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.090
We are able to express
ourselves, our language,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.930
that's within deaf culture, but
we're also damn good actors.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.165
So that's all you could say.

00:03:57.165 --> 00:03:57.998
- Yeah. Yeah.
- I mean,

00:03:57.998 --> 00:03:59.370
when you're doing (indistinct).

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:00.858
(audience applauds)

00:04:00.858 --> 00:04:05.858
But this movie is one that most people,

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.240
the majority of people
probably aren't accustomed to.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.960
I mean, there are other kinds of movies

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.630
and those are all wonderful films,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.830
but this is unique and
it's special in itself.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.860
And the fact that there's
so many different levels

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.770
operating at the same time in the film,

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:19.340
people will connect to whatever level

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.740
that they're watching in the film.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.130
Whether it's fishing, or songs, or music,

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.160
or sign language, or
schools, or teenage life,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.350
or family life, it's all in one film.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.820
It's the entire package.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.300
It's a beautiful, wonderful
package for the audience.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.320
- I couldn't agree more,

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.080
and we talked so much about inclusion

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.550
and nobody has ever talked
about this before and around me.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.930
So I'm just curious, like Troy, Daniel,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.760
y'all like what kind of
movie would you love to do?

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.450
Like what kind of movies
would you love to see now?

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.020
This is like more introduced
to pop culture now,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.550
and I feel like hearing a story

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:53.820
that maybe we've heard before

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.173
but from a different vantage
point is so important.

00:04:59.016 --> 00:05:00.953
- [Daniel's Translator] Well, I mean,

00:05:00.953 --> 00:05:01.786
we have so many stories.

00:05:01.786 --> 00:05:04.750
We have so many more stories
about deaf culture to tell.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.840
We really can, and we
can play any character.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.840
And I would love to see
more deaf superheroes,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.210
and maybe adding more characters

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.200
like a deaf character in "Star Wars."

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.782
- I know, that was
amazing, "The Mandalorian."

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:17.937
"The Mandalorian," it's amazing.

00:05:17.937 --> 00:05:19.456
- [Daniel's Translator] He's the first

00:05:19.456 --> 00:05:20.289
deaf person on "Star Wars."

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:22.835
So it's a huge honor and I applaud him.

00:05:22.835 --> 00:05:24.688
- [Troy's Translator] There's
so many things we have.

00:05:24.688 --> 00:05:26.460
(audience applauds)

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.810
The deaf community has so a
rich history of storytelling.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.670
And so I would like to just plant the seed

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.240
and share these stories.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.930
There were historical
stories in slavery times,

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.320
there's a lot of deaf history

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.380
and so we can be able
to pick those carrots

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.450
and bring them forward.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.830
And so I really want to
change everyone's perspective

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.130
and show everyone what
deaf people went through,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.910
because many folks don't understand.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.920
And thanks to "CODA",
it speaks for itself.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.330
And it's increasing awareness
of the deaf community

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.770
and we're bridging two worlds,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.280
the hearing and the deaf world,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.070
for which for so long were separate.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.410
So we focused on one side,
we focused on the other,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:04.470
and where is the center?

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.770
The center is the "CODA."

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.140
And so Emilia was the center of our story

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.411
that pulled both of these
communities together.

00:06:10.411 --> 00:06:11.733
(audience applauds)

00:06:11.733 --> 00:06:14.440
- So is it true that you, Emilia,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.950
you had to learn to sign, fish, sing,

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:17.800
and do an American accent.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.040
You did all the things for this film.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.570
Like most actors aren't
asked to do all of that.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:23.760
- No, I know. I know.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.060
There was a big kind of by
every skill that was required,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:29.660
I just had a no, no, no, no,
no, and I was 17 and British.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.000
And so I really didn't think

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.840
it was gonna work out for me
when I was auditioning, but-

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.310
- It was almost as if they were like,

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:36.750
let's like pick a person that's just

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.950
we're gonna torture and
have to do all these things.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.806
Like I was like, that is a
lot of things all at once.

00:06:41.806 --> 00:06:42.639
- It was, it was.

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:43.562
It was a lot.

00:06:43.562 --> 00:06:44.395
I was also doing my A
levels at the time too.

00:06:44.395 --> 00:06:46.960
So having to get 15
hours of schoolwork in,

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.940
it was a lot of work and
it was very, very hard

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.040
but it was also so rewarding.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:51.873
- Absolutely.

00:06:51.873 --> 00:06:52.980
I can't imagine this is your first film.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:57.030
What a beautiful piece of
history to be a part of

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.480
as your first experience.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.313
- I feel so...

00:06:59.313 --> 00:07:01.130
I will honestly be forever
grateful and honored

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:02.710
to have been a part of this film.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.600
- Yeah, I will always be impressed.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.330
I was like the fact that I learned

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.090
that you did not know sign language before

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.470
I was like, wait, what?

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:10.460
I'm such a loser.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:11.600
I can learn this.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:12.810
I was like, I can do this.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.320
If she can do this, I can do this.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.130
So Daniel, you and Troy were roommates

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.730
while shooting the movie, right?

00:07:18.679 --> 00:07:20.103
- [Daniel's Translator] Oh yeah, we were.

00:07:20.103 --> 00:07:21.684
Yes, we were.

00:07:21.684 --> 00:07:22.517
- I gotta be honest with you.

00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:23.350
That had to be one hell of an experience

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:24.320
'cause he seems like fun.

00:07:26.654 --> 00:07:27.870
- [Daniel's Translator]
Obviously, he is a lot of fun.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:28.703
You can see.

00:07:30.166 --> 00:07:31.140
- [Troy's Translator] Daniel and I,

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:32.460
so we wanted to be roommates

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.490
because they were going
to separate us in a hotel.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:37.950
And we really wanted to
benefit from the experience

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.220
of being roommates like father and son.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.660
And we were able to chat,
how to pick up women,

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.810
and advice I'd give him about clothes,

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.340
and different types of
fishing boats, and so on.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:51.940
And so we moved into an
Airbnb together as roommates,

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.240
and we were really excited.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:55.220
You could see the water and the boats

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:56.370
going by in the harbor.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.620
It was really cool.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.380
And at night we were trying
to switch all the lights on.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:03.130
And so we saw these lights
slowly starting to switch on,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.740
and we went outside

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.182
and we sat on the patio.
- And it was beautiful.

00:08:06.182 --> 00:08:07.390
- [Troy's Translator] Then what happened?

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:08.420
- [Daniel's Translator]
It looked beautiful.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:09.760
So we're just looking around.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.240
We were so excited, we're
here at the "CODA" movie.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.680
We're discussing all of our ideas

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.720
and then, you know, we
can see a cop pull up

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.460
with strobe lights and he
walks up to the front door.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.240
And really the cops are here.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.770
So Troy and I get up and we're nervous

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:24.640
and we run downstairs.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:25.740
We're deaf, we can't hear,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.660
we're telling them from the balcony.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.690
And they're like, okay, and
they walk up to the front door.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:31.023
We let 'em in the house.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.276
We start writing back
and forth with the cop.

00:08:34.276 --> 00:08:35.280
- [Troy's Translator]
So they said there is

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.110
a blaring fog horn from your house

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:39.743
that's been going off for hours.

00:08:40.699 --> 00:08:41.532
And we said, really?

00:08:41.532 --> 00:08:44.040
So the neighbors were
complaining and calling the cops

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:44.873
and we didn't know.

00:08:44.873 --> 00:08:47.477
We were just enjoying ourselves
as the fog horn was blaring.

00:08:47.477 --> 00:08:49.534
And we didn't know how to switch it off.

00:08:49.534 --> 00:08:50.750
- [Daniel's Translator] I
mean, we felt so peaceful.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:53.440
We can feel bass, but we
couldn't hear that fog horn.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.560
We had no idea.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:56.460
We were having the most
beautiful peaceful night,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.683
but really we were
destroying our neighbors.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:00.680
- [Troy's Translator] Yeah, and so we were

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.490
trying to figure out how to switch it off.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.510
And so we were trying
all the light switches

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.220
and they looked all the same.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.690
So one of the light switches
was actually the fog horn.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.869
So that's where the big screw up was.

00:09:11.869 --> 00:09:12.702
- Oh my.

00:09:12.702 --> 00:09:13.580
And we need to mention everybody

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.730
that Troy is the first deaf male actor

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.207
to earn an Oscar nomination and to win

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:21.216
an individual SAG award.

00:09:21.216 --> 00:09:22.386
(audience cheers)

00:09:22.386 --> 00:09:24.670
I mean, it's exciting.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.033
So how does that feel?

00:09:27.411 --> 00:09:29.580
- [Troy's Translator] Of
course I'm extremely excited,

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.540
and it's great to finally be recognized.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.560
It means that our peers are
accepting our work as artists.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:38.480
Not because we're deaf, we're just actors

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.240
who happen to be deaf

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.190
and we're being recognized for our talent.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:43.600
And so I'm so glad.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.770
It's been a long and tough journey for me.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.380
I feel like I've received an honorary PhD

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.240
when getting these nominations,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.430
and it's like I finally
arrived at their level

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:53.777
and I feel so blessed.

00:09:53.777 --> 00:09:56.750
(audience applauds)

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.400
You know, the decisions
that I made in my life

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.620
brought me here, but Marlee was
such an important role model

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:03.740
and she kept me alive.

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.420
She gave Daniel and I hope.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.520
And so Marley was a great representative

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.480
for the deaf community for so many years,

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:12.200
and now we're able to come up

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.042
and so I'm so grateful to Marlee for that.

00:10:14.042 --> 00:10:15.470
(audience applauds)

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:16.428
- [Daniel's Translator] I agree.

00:10:16.428 --> 00:10:18.160
- It's so interesting 'cause
Marlee we've seen in movies.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.340
I've seen movies, TV, I've
seen Marlee in so many things

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:23.050
and it's like, it's one of
those things where it's like,

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.664
why are we just seeing like one person?

00:10:25.664 --> 00:10:26.497
Like, she's amazing,

00:10:26.497 --> 00:10:27.990
but like there has to
be a whole community.

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.890
And I feel like Marlee
was very important for you

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:31.970
to be a part of this movie in particular,

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:34.490
because it not only
showcases like your gifts

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.360
your talent as an actor,
but also to help highlight

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.890
other actors that just happen to be deaf

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:42.060
that love what they do
and excel at what they do.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.160
Is this such an important project for you?

00:10:45.247 --> 00:10:46.080
- [Marlee's Translator] Absolutely.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.580
I mean, it's been 35 years
being in the industry

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.700
that I've tried to spread the
message to people that yes,

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.530
I'm here, but I'm not the only one.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:56.010
There are so many other
deaf actors out there,

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.480
deaf directors, deaf writers, and Codas.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.670
People who can sign and who can act.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:06.870
And it's sometimes, you know,
would fall on deaf ears.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:10.000
That sounds ironic, but
it takes collaboration.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.290
It takes one to listen,
to spread the message.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:16.290
It takes work, it takes homework.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.170
But at the end of the day, we are here,

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.910
we're focused on doing
whatever it is we can do.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.590
We can continue to make noise,

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:27.380
but the key is collaboration.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:29.573
It's collaboration in all ways.

